// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: meta-parameters, refinement, declared-but-unenforced
// @expected-error: E506
// @description: A refinement on a META PARAMETER is enforced, at
//   compile time, which is the point of a compile-time parameter.
//
//     type Digest<N: meta USize {it == 4 || it == 8}> is …;
//     let bad: Digest<5> = …;
//
//     E506: meta argument 1 of `Digest` violates its refinement:
//           `5` does not satisfy `it == 4 || it == 8`
//
// It was accepted in silence. The grammar admits the refinement, the
// parser stored it, and every reader of the meta-parameter kind
// destructured it away with `{ name, .. }` — a feature present,
// accepted, documented, and doing nothing, so everything built on it
// inherited a guarantee that was never made.
//
// A digest width is exactly what someone would lean on: mixing a
// 32-byte and a 64-byte digest is a real supply-chain bug class, and
// this reads like the language rules it out.
//
// TWO PLACES carried the predicate and only one was the field named for
// it: `N: meta USize {it == 4}` is parsed by `parse_type` reaching the
// brace first, so it lands on the TYPE as a `Refined` wrapper while the
// `refinement` field stays empty. Reading only the field found nothing.
//
// `meta_parameter_identity.vr` is the other pole: the width is part of
// TYPE IDENTITY, and a `Digest<8>` where a `Digest<4>` is wanted is
// refused for a different reason. The two together say how far the
// feature reaches.

public type Digest<N: meta USize {it == 4 || it == 8}> is {
    bytes: [Byte; N],
};

fn main() {
    // 5 is not admitted by the predicate.
    let bad: Digest<5> = Digest { bytes: [1_u8, 2_u8, 3_u8, 4_u8, 5_u8] };
    print(f"len={bad.bytes.len()}");
}
